{
  "version": 5,
  "name": "How-Ohms-Law-Works-Resistor-And-Pot-v3",
  "description": "Two separate loops share one 5 volt supply, so each resistor sees the full 5 volts. The first loop holds a 1 kilohm fixed resistor, which carries 5 volts divided by 1,000 ohms, or 5 milliamps (thousandths of an amp). The second loop holds a potentiometer (a variable resistor with a knob) set to 5 kilohms, which carries 1 milliamp. Ohm's law says current equals voltage divided by resistance, so changing the pot's setting in its Properties card changes its current in a way you can predict and then check with the multimeter.",
  "wheelLocked": false,
